WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.750
A next question.

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:07.879
Hey David, if a Pete Buddha judge wins Iowa,
could he win the nomination even if he never

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:11.110
takes the lead in national polling?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.960
This is a very interesting question.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.580
So if you look at national polling right now,
as of as of this recording, booted judges

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.050
polling just under 8%.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.420
Now that doesn't sound like much, but it does
represent a close to doubling of Pete booted

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.670
judges polling.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.640
If you go back just a few months, Pete, who?

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:34.130
Jesuit polling as low a as as about 4% he's
at nearly 8% so certainly his national support

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.230
has increased.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.960
He has been helped, as we talked about earlier
by MSNBC.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:44.950
His choice to give him way more talk time
than his polling level would dictate if talk

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.250
time correlates with polling support.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.820
But this is an election that is a bunch of
primaries.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.050
It is not one national election.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.289
So let's look at the States.

00:00:54.289 --> 00:01:01.829
If you look at Iowa, a Pete Buddha judge,
according to the last four polls, is leading

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:08.150
in Iowa and he surged to take the lead over
Elizabeth Warren, Bernie Sanders, and Joe

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.250
Biden, Warren Sanders and Biden, depending
on which poll, you look at our between second

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.090
and fourth P Buddha judges in the lead.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.250
What about New Hampshire?

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.490
If you go over to New Hampshire right now,
Pete Buddha judge has made a significant gain

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:27.310
in New Hampshire but is not as of now in the
lead, technically still in third place, although

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.259
within the margin of error ahead of Bernie,
behind Warren and Biden.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:38.600
Then you go to Nevada, which is primary number
three and in Nevada, Buddha judge at this

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.890
point appears to have no chance whatsoever.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.990
He's pulling around five to nine and remember
that if you don't get 15% you don't get any

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:45.990
delegates whatsoever.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.130
So in Nevada as of now, shaping up to be a
zero for Buddha judge.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.869
And then when you look at South Carolina,
the first of the four primaries that has more

00:01:54.869 --> 00:02:00.600
minority voters, Pete, Buddha, judge, also
very behind in fourth place with as little

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.790
as 3% support in one of the most recent polls.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.350
So if Pete Buddha judge wins Iowa, can he
win the nomination?

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:12.660
It is not obvious to me, but he also is still
in this thing.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.810
There are people who are big budaj supporters
who are making the case that he is sort of

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:22.730
on an Obama like trajectory for late gain
and that even if he's not winning right now

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:28.060
in Nevada and South Carolina, if he takes
Iowa, that's going to help him in New Hampshire.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.170
If you take Iowa and New Hampshire that's
going to get a momentum and help them in Nevada

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.230
and then at least he might be able to get
some delegates out of South Carolina.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.430
Even as unlikely as it might be that he wins
there.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:46.190
The answer is yeah, he could win, but it is
a significantly uphill battle compared to

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:54.610
Biden, Warren and Bernie who are currently
in a position where if their elections today

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.910
mirrored their polling today, they would get
delegates.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:02.580
Pete, Buddha, judge in a number of these States
would get zero delegates because he's not

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.879
pulling 15% which is required to get any delegates.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:11.340
Iowa's a little different because it's a caucus
where there are sort of subsequent rounds

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.010
of consolidating support.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.320
We don't have to get into the details of that.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.390
So if you are a Buddha judge supporter and
you are holding out hope, yes he could still

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.390
win the nomination.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.040
And this is why I've said it does make sense
for him to be in the debates.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.250
It doesn't make sense for a lot of the other
people who are in the debates to be in the

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:29.250
debates.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:34.400
If you are not a Buddha judge supporter, it
is still the most likely scenario that he

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.440
does not secure the nomination.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.769
But it was unlikely that Trump was going to
secure the nomination at one point as well.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:43.299
So let's not read more into that than is warranted.

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:47.450
Uh, he's in this thing and if he does win
Iowa, it will no doubt be a boost to his campaign.

